{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"426\t/**\n427\t * Convert a line that is not a `user`/`assistant` turn into the record it\n428\t * deserves, or return null when the type is genuinely unknown (the caller\n429\t * then routes it to the drift path).\n430\t *\n431\t * `system` lines split on whether they carry text. Claude Code uses the type\n432\t * for two unrelated things: printing into the conversation (a slash command's\n433\t * stdout, an away summary, a refusal fallback — all of which the user read,\n434\t * so they are turns spoken by the system) and recording harness bookkeeping\n435\t * (turn durations, hook summaries — which nobody read, so they are activity).\n436\t * A string `content` field is exactly the difference, so that is the test,\n437\t * rather than a subtype list that would go stale the moment Claude Code adds\n438\t * a subtype.\n439\t */\n440\tfunction convertRecordLine(line: ClaudeTranscriptLine, ctx: RecordContext): EventDraft | null {\n441\t  const type = line.type;\n442\t  if (typeof type !== \"string\") return null;\n443\t  const fields = payloadFields(line as unknown as Record<string, unknown>);\n444\t\n445\t  if (type === \"system\") {\n446\t    if (typeof line.content === \"string\" && line.content.trim() !== \"\") {\n447\t      return recordDraft(\n448\t        ctx,\n449\t        \"conversation_turn\",\n450\t        \"system\",\n451\t        { ...liftText(fields, \"content\"), role: \"system\" },\n452\t        line,\n453\t      );\n454\t    }\n455\t    return activityDraft(ctx, `system/${line.subtype ?? \"untyped\"}`, fields, line);\n456\t  }\n457\t\n458\t  if (type === \"attachment\") {\n459\t    const attachmentType = line.attachment?.type;\n460\t    return contextInjectionDraft(\n461\t      ctx,\n462\t      `attachment/${typeof attachmentType === \"string\" ? attachmentType : \"untyped\"}`,\n463\t      fields,\n464\t      line,\n465\t    );\n466\t  }\n4