inked.\n\nBoth CLIs were installed and driven against a local OpenAI-compatible endpoint to produce real sessions; every format claim below comes from a live transcript or from the CLI's own shipped source, not inference.\n\n## Gemini CLI\n\n`~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl`, hooked on `AfterAgent` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nThe file is append-only but is **not** a transcript — it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document: `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence.\n\n**It deliberately does not honour the removals.** On a real session, Gemini rewrote the document with a snapshot that silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed (after a failed API call), so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value.\n\nWith 0.19.0's `activity` kind available, the rewind is now recorded *itself*, naming the messages it withdrew — so a withdrawn turn is legible as withdrawn rather than merely present. That closes the gap the first version of this PR could only list as a roadmap item.\n\nEverything else that is not a turn is recorded too: `$set` patches become `session_state`, and `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Sub-sessions filed under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` are captured as sub-conversations with `conversation.parent` set.\n\nBecause nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting — over messages and mutation records alike, so they share one number space and cannot collide — and never reused. A rewind therefore cannot renumber later turns; the positional-`seq` churn the opencode adapter documents and accepts does not arise here.\n\n## Qwen Code\n\n`~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl`, hooked on `Stop` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nA fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so this adapter is that adapter's twin — per-line conversion, line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, and the same catch-up sweep for stranded transcript tails.\n\nEverything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`, and each maps to a kind:\n\n| subtype | kind |\n|---|---|\n| `attribution_snapshot` | `file_snapshot` — per-file content hashes are already **in** the record, so unlike Claude Code's file history nothing needs resolving against a machine-local cache and `resolved` stays empty |\n| `custom_title`, `session_source`, `parent_session`, `goal_state` | `session_state` |\n| `at_command`, `agent_bootstrap`, `agent_launch_prompt` | `context_injection` |\n| everything else, including subtypes a future Qwen adds | `activity` |\n\nThe type list is Qwen's own `RECORD_TYPES` (`user`/`assistant`/`tool_result`/`system`), read out of its shipped sources, so only a genuinely new top-level type routes to the drift path. A `parent_session` record makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation of the session that spawned it.\n\nBoth CLIs express content as Google GenAI `Part` lists, so that conversion lives once in `adapters/genai-parts.ts`.\n\n## Shared cursor, and a bug fix for codex\n\n0.19.0 taught the claude-code cursor three things the other adapters still did not know: **absent is not zero** (a missing cursor gates the sweep), **`size` bounds the sweep**, and **the write must be atomic** (two writers). Each fix was worth having everywhere rather than in one of five copies, so the cursor moved into `adapters/common.ts` and all five adapters now use it — field names preserved per adapter, so no cursor on disk resets.\n\nThat also surfaced that **codex still had the torn-trailing-line bug** 0.19.0 fixed for claude-code: the cursor advanced past a half-written line, so the completed line was never captured. Fixed with the same narrow rule (unterminated file *and* final line) — holding at any parse failure would stall a session's capture forever on real corruption.\n\n## One ugly-but-necessary detail\n\n`install` appends `>/dev/null 2>&1` to the hook command for these two CLIs only. Verified on Qwen Code 0.21.5: `qwen -p \"...\"` fires its Stop hook and immediately exits, killing the hook process; a deliberately slow probe hook reproduced it every time. The redirection fixes it reliably, at the cost of the capture no longer printing drift warnings where you can see them — the same trade the opencode plugin already makes. Documented in the Roadmap; the real fix belongs upstream.\n\n## Verification\n\n- 197 tests, 196 pass / 1 pre-existing skip. 16 new (6 qwen, 10 gemini).\n- Real Qwen sessions captured: **46 turns, 52 activity, 20 file_snapshot** — nothing dropped, no drift warnings.\n- End to end through the installed hook: a live `qwen` run editing a file produced 6 turns, 2 file snapshots and 5 activity records, with the machinery kinds correctly hidden from the default `log` view.\n- The real Gemini session captured both messages including the one faithful replay would have lost, plus its metadata patches as `session_state`, with correct `system` vs `human` attribution.\n\n## Known gaps (in the Roadmap)\n\n- Gemini stamps no CLI version into its session file, so gemini-cli events have no `producer.source_version` unless a caller supplies one. Reading it off the installed binary would be wrong for a backfill.\n- Qwen `system` subtypes are classified by name from its own `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES` list; only two appear in the sessions on hand. Fields pass through verbatim either way, so the risk is a record filed under a slightly wrong kind, not lost content.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Ewo7xu7kzLpH4MeEDK5LDc\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/8f1a9339/tmp/prbody.md","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[],"type":"create","userModified":false},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"ed3884b8-3ce7-4454-9020-a97995fa616c","version":"2.1.223"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-06T01:44:21.078Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
