{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01NrXLQzgsaiLxomjMb9WriM","input":{"content":"p = 'README.md'\ns = open(p).read()\n\n# --- adapter table rows -------------------------------------------------\nanchor = \"\\n\\nTODO adapters\"\ni = s.index(anchor)\nnew_rows = \"\"\"\n| Gemini CLI | `AfterAgent`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl` | The session file is append-only but is *not* a transcript: it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document — `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence but deliberately does **not** honour the removals: verified on a live session, Gemini rewrote the document after a failed API call and silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed, so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value, and records the `$rewindTo` itself as an `activity` naming what it withdrew — so a withdrawn turn is legible as withdrawn rather than merely present. `$set` patches become `session_state`, `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Because nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting and never reused, so a rewind cannot renumber later turns — the positional churn the opencode adapter accepts does not arise here. A model turn whose tool calls have not settled is withheld and holds the cursor back. Sub-sessions filed under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` are captured as sub-conversations. Gemini's harness-authored `<session_context>`/`<hook_context>` preambles are recorded but attributed to `system`, not to you. |\n| Qwen Code | `Stop`/`SessionE